**Battle of Didgori**

The 19th-century anonymous Georgian oil painting of the Battle of Didgori (1121), held by the British Museum, inaccurately depicts Queen Tamar of Georgia (r. 1184–1213) as an empress on horseback firing a pistol—an anachronism, as she was not present and firearms did not exist then—and shows Saint George in the sky hurling a spear at the defeated Seljuk army. These fantastical elements reflect folkloric embellishments common in later Georgian art.

\boxed{Battle of Didgori}

**Toulouse**

During the 1217–1218 Siege of Toulouse in the Albigensian Crusade, the city defended Cathars ("these people") by building the trebuchet *Malvoisine*, which destroyed a crusader siege tower called "the Cat." This event inspired Raimon Escrivan's Occitan poem *Lo cat e la trebuchet*, a humorous debate between the two machines.

\boxed{Toulouse}
